.TH AMIGAUX-RELEASE 4 "17 November 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
amigaux-release \- which Amigaux release this system is
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/amigaux-release
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B /etc/amigaux-release
names the Amigaux release the system was installed or last updated to, on
its one line:
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
amigaux 1.1.2
.ft R
.fi
.PP
The first word is always
.BR amigaux ;
the second is the release, numbers separated by dots: the release (1.1),
or a point release of it (1.1.1, 1.1.2).
A program that wants the release reads the second word of the first line.
.PP
The base package ships the file, written when the package is built from
the release's version (the file
.B media/VERSION
of the source tree, from which the release media take their names too).
So every release and point release carries a new revision of the base
package, and updating to a point release brings this file with it.
The file is not a configuration file: an update
.RB ( "apkg upgrade" )
replaces it with the new release's, so it is not to be edited.
.PP
.B "uname \-v"
and
.B "uname \-r"
still give the system's own version and release, 2.1c and 4.0; the kernel
in use is in the first line the system prints at boot and in
.BR "uname \-a" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/amigaux-release
this file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apkg (1),
.BR uname (1)
.SH NOTES
A system installed from the 1.1 or 1.1.1 media, with a base package before
1.0-37, has no such file until its base package is updated.
